WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.656
Temporary files should be treated as sensitive whenever their contents or
metadata are sensitive.

2
00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:18.179
Uploads, generated documents, exports, reports, converted media, decrypted
intermediate data, personal data, regulated records, credentials,
certificates, and private keys can all appear in temporary workflows.

3
00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:26.191
The fact that the file is temporary does not reduce the impact if the
wrong user, process, log bundle, backup, or support artifact receives it.

4
00:00:26.191 --> 00:00:29.423
Sensitive-data minimization is the strongest control.

5
00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:35.684
If a workflow can process data in memory safely and reliably, it may not
need to write sensitive content to disk.

6
00:00:35.684 --> 00:00:47.197
If disk staging is required, write the smallest necessary data, store it
in an approved location, restrict permissions, avoid unnecessary copies,
and remove it as soon as the workflow no longer needs it.

7
00:00:47.197 --> 00:00:53.526
Avoid writing secrets to temporary files unless there is a carefully
justified design and a clear cleanup path.

8
00:00:53.526 --> 00:01:00.663
Encryption at rest can help in some architectures, but it is not a reason
to ignore lifecycle, permissions, or exposure paths.

9
00:01:00.663 --> 00:01:08.675
A temporary file can still be exposed through logs, crash reports, support
bundles, debug output, backups, snapshots, or copied artifacts.

10
00:01:08.675 --> 00:01:12.446
Filenames and directory names can also reveal sensitive context.

11
00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:18.976
Avoid putting secrets, personal identifiers, tenant names, or private
business terms into temporary filenames.

12
00:01:18.976 --> 00:01:28.133
Privacy-aware temporary storage asks not only who can read the file, but
also who can infer meaning from its existence, name, location, metadata,
and lifecycle.

13
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:32.038
A report staged for one customer should not reveal itself to another.

14
00:01:32.038 --> 00:01:36.011
A processing failure should not preserve private data indefinitely.

15
00:01:36.011 --> 00:01:43.080
Temporary storage should support the business workflow without becoming an
unmanaged side channel for sensitive content.
